From: Tian Zheng <zhengtian10@huawei.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 16:34:05 +0800
Subject: [PATCH v5 00/15] KVM: arm64: FEAT_HDBSS support for stage-2 dirty
 tracking

This series implements hardware-assisted stage-2 dirty tracking on
arm64 using FEAT_HDBSS, and is the combined series of Leonardo Bras'
HAFDBS descriptors rework [1] and the HDBSS buffer support built on
top of it, as requested during the review of [1].

The first five patches are the HAFDBS base from Leonardo's RFC,
reworked per the review feedback and folded with fixes developed
while validating the HDBSS work on top: the descriptor encoding moves
the stage-2 write permission from S2AP[1] to DBM and reuses S2AP[1]
as the dirty state, giving the three states the rest of the series
builds on:

    RO  (DBM=0, S2AP[1]=0)  read-only, write -> permission fault
    WC  (DBM=1, S2AP[1]=0)  writable-clean, hw promotes on write
    WD  (DBM=1, S2AP[1]=1)  writable-dirty

The remaining patches add the HDBSS buffer machinery: per-vCPU
buffers, the buffer-full fault handling, draining the buffers into
the dirty bitmap or the dirty ring on every VM exit, and a single
derived hardware-dirty mode so that:

    - migration with HDBSS  -> HD|HA|HDBSS, hardware tracks dirtiness
    - migration without     -> HD off, classic write-protect faults
    - no migration          -> HD|HA, only written pages go dirty

A note on nested virtualization: HAFDBS is not gated on NV, since
shadow stage-2 MMUs build their own VTCR through kvm_get_vtcr(),
which never carries the HD bit - the canonical stage-2 of a guest
hypervisor runs under hardware management while L2 keeps its
software-managed behaviour, and the L1-visible HAFDBS ID field is
limited to AF-only so a guest kernel never reaches for the feature
itself. HDBSS, in contrast, stays gated on NV for now: recording L1
dirtiness into the HDBSS buffer while L2 runs against a shadow needs
the nested exit-flush and harvest paths audited first. The nested
walker deliberately consumes only S2AP[1] for writability; DBM (bit
51) is RES0 from the L1 point of view and must not be interpreted
regardless of the guest's VTCR_EL2.HD value.

The HDBSS buffer registers are programmed based on buffer ownership
rather than feature enablement (patch 7): HDBSS can be turned on in
VTCR_EL2 while a vCPU is mid-KVM_RUN, and hardware dirty-state
updates write dirtied IPAs to HDBSSBR_EL2.BADDR without any fault,
so the registers must already point at the running vCPU's buffer by
then. This was validated the hard way: with the registers only
programmed while HDBSS was enabled, a 4K-stage2 dirty-ring migration
under redis load corrupted destination memory through a stale buffer
address (entries landing in freed-and-reused pages, dirty records
lost), and the ownership-based programming removes that window
entirely.

Functional coverage: the KVM_CAP_ARM_HDBSS_BUFFER_SIZE interface is
exercised by a new selftest (patch 15), including the contract that
dirty-ring mode pins the buffer size to the default. The 4K + dirty
ring migration scenario above was additionally validated under a
redis workload; performance numbers for the non-migration HAFDBS
fault-elision path are being collected and will be posted as a
follow-up to this series.

No userspace changes are required beyond optionally setting
KVM_CAP_ARM_HDBSS_BUFFER_SIZE; a QEMU tree carrying the adapted
dirty-ring plumbing exists for testing.

Comments welcome, especially on:

  - the ownership-based HDBSS register programming (patch 7) vs.
    programming them at each VTCR-flipping site,
  - keeping HDBSS NV-gated while HAFDBS is not (patch 12),
  - the per-MMU kvm_hw_dirty_enabled() predicate (patch 12), which
    keeps shadow installs writable-dirty so nested behaviour is
    unchanged.

[1] https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260901171558.2674031-1-leo.bras@arm.com/

